verall transformation is supported (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu/H2S-mediated reductive deoxygenation of hydroxyacetone to acetone\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental copper\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"elemental copper\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic cyanosulfidic pool; hydroxyacetone available directly or from upstream sugar chemistry; balanced graph implicitly uses multiple sulfide equivalents.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Copper-assisted reductive removal of the hydroxymethyl oxygen from hydroxyacetone to give acetone.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the alternative upstream entry reported in the reviewed cyanosulfidic valine chemistry. Patel et al. (2015) describe hydroxyacetone reduction to acetone under Cu/H2S conditions. As in rxn_003, H2S2 is made explicit here as conservative sulfur-redox bookkeeping rather than a uniquely established sulfur sink for this specific step (Patel et al., 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin formation from acetone\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cyanide-rich anoxic surface pool; concentration enhanced during evaporation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic addition of cyanide to the ketone carbonyl followed by protonation of the alkoxide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Directly supported in the Patel cyanosulfidic network: cyanide adds to acetone to give 